+    <section id="gan-en-loss-detail"><h4 id="gan-en-loss">Implemented losses and a numerical example</h4>
+    <p>For the default log loss, x is a real sample, z is noise, and each mean is taken over the batch. The 0.95 multiplies the discriminator's real-sample term.</p>
+    <p><code>L_D = −[0.95 × mean(log D(x)) + mean(log(1 − D(G(z))))]</code><br /><code>L_G = −mean(log D(G(z)))</code></p>
+    <p>For one real and one fake sample with D(x)=0.8 and D(G(z))=0.2, the natural logarithm gives discriminator loss −[0.95 log(0.8) + log(0.8)] ≈ <strong>0.435</strong> and generator loss −log(0.2) ≈ <strong>1.609</strong>. The discriminator minimizes both terms; the generator minimizes its own loss, favoring a higher fake score.</p>
+    <p>Selecting LeastSq loss changes both losses: <code>L_D = mean((D(x) − 1)²) + mean(D(G(z))²)</code>, <code>L_G = mean((D(G(z)) − 1)²)</code>. For the same single-sample scores, the results are <strong>0.08</strong> and <strong>0.64</strong>.</p></section>
     
+    <p id="gan-en-loss-display">The lab’s loss numbers and loss chart show the objectives above. When there are multiple updates, the recorded value is the loss on the batch used for the final update, before that update. Discriminator and generator losses can use different batches and be calculated at different times. When only one model is updated, the other number retains its previous value and its chart has no point for that step. The initial pretrained view evaluates a fixed example batch. Bar lengths use different display scaling for each model and loss type, so use the numbers and chart when comparing values.</p>
+    <h4 id="gan-en-metrics">Reading the distribution metrics</h4>
+    <p>In addition to the training losses, the right-hand chart compares real and generated samples in grid cells. KL measures a difference from the real distribution P to the generated distribution Q; JS compares each distribution with their average M=(P+Q)/2. A smaller score indicates a closer match on this grid, not proof of a good generator.</p>
+    <p><code>KL(P‖Q) = Σ Pᵢ log₂(Pᵢ/Qᵢ)</code><br /><code>JS(P,Q) = ½ KL(P‖M) + ½ KL(Q‖M)</code></p>
+    <p>For two cells, P=[0.75, 0.25] and Q=[0.5, 0.5] give M=[0.625, 0.375], KL≈0.189 and JS≈0.049. This illustration has no smoothing. The chart uses a 30×30 grid, finite samples, and adds 0.0001 to each cell without renormalizing. With at least one sample from each distribution, each smoothed array sums to 1.09. The chart values therefore differ from the theoretical KL and JS of normalized probability distributions. Points outside the display area are assigned to the nearest edge cell.</p>
